course: dictionary
lesson: 7773
title: One small word, many meanings: put
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Put is a small word with a big job. It is a verb, which means it is an action word, and its forms are puts, put and putting. Most of the time it means to move something to a place. Look at this: «A seat is for a person, so put your bag on your lap or the floor when the bus is busy.» The bag is moved, so put means to place it.
explain: Small words that follow put change its meaning. Put on, put away, put off and put up all mean different things. Put on is for clothes, put away is for tidying things like toys, and put off means to delay something. When you meet put with another small word, ask what the sentence is about, then choose the meaning that fits.
explain: Put can also mean to write or say something. Here the author means «put it simply» as in «say it in an easy way», so nothing is moved at all. Context clues help you spot this. Check the words around put before you decide what it means.
explain: Put can also be a noun in shot put, a sport where you throw a heavy ball as far as you can. Look for clue words like sport, ball and throw. Context tells you which kind of put you are reading.
fact: Put is a verb, which means it is an action word. Its forms are puts, put and putting.
fact: «Put» means to move something to a place, such as «put the cup on the table».
fact: «Put on» can mean to wear clothes, and «put off» means to delay something until later.
fact: «Stay put» means stay where you are, and «put your feet up» means rest.
fact: Shot put is a sport where you throw a heavy ball as far as you can.
exercise: «A seat is for a person, so put your bag on your lap or the floor when the bus is busy.» What does put mean here? | To delay something until later | To wear something | *To move something to a place | To write something down
exercise: «Next day he asks politely and plays where they put him.» What does put mean here? | To say something in a simple way | To switch something off | *To place someone in a spot | To tidy something away
exercise: Which phrase means to delay something until later? | put up | *put off | put on | put away
test: «Saving means putting money aside, week after week, until you have enough for something bigger.» What does putting money aside mean? | *To keep it to one side to save it | To spend it all at once | To throw it away | To count it again
test: «Clearing the name of the person you blamed is part of putting it right.» What does putting it right mean? | To put it off | *To make it correct again | To say it is wrong | To put it in a box
exam: Which formal word could replace put in «Put the cups on the table»? | puzzle | pour | *place | pause
